00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:36.660
And the importance of aim trainers can't be
overlooked for this reason...

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:40.760
So if you've hesitated to use aim-trainers
in the past, wondering if they are really

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:43.760
worth your time, then take notes.

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:46.740
They can provide a significant advantage for
you.

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:52.790
Not only as a warm-up tool but a method for
actively improving your skills.

00:03:52.790 --> 00:03:58.510
But just by using aim labs, Kovaaks, or any
other aim trainer, you won't become an instant

00:03:58.510 --> 00:03:59.890
aim-god.

00:03:59.890 --> 00:04:05.180
And so I wanted to figure out the different
factors that hold players back from getting

00:04:05.180 --> 00:04:06.820
good aim.

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:12.420
When thinking deeply about this problem, it's
common for many players to chalk it up to

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:16.440
genetics... and for a good reason.

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:22.710
Let's say you and your best friend both start
playing a new FPS game, and each has never

00:04:22.710 --> 00:04:26.090
played with a mouse and keyboard in the past.

00:04:26.090 --> 00:04:29.210
Chances are you'll both suck with your aiming.

00:04:29.210 --> 00:04:34.040
But for some reason, your friend might have
a faster baseline reaction time or quicker

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:37.290
hands, which allows them to do better than
you.

00:04:37.290 --> 00:04:41.990
It's easy to label this as a genetic advantage
and assume that they will always be a little

00:04:41.990 --> 00:04:43.460
bit better than you.

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:45.170
This is what we usually think.

00:04:45.170 --> 00:04:47.090
But this is a flawed assumption.

00:04:47.090 --> 00:04:53.290
In reality, a head start doesn't mean they
will always have an advantage; in fact, the

00:04:53.290 --> 00:04:57.020
factor that will really predict improvement
is your mindset...

00:04:57.020 --> 00:05:04.680
RRKim: I think, I mean how much time and thought
you put into something, too, will impact your

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:05.730
improvement rate.

00:05:05.730 --> 00:05:10.530
So someone if the most important thing to
them is getting good at aiming, they'll figure

00:05:10.530 --> 00:05:15.580
out a way they'll put the hours in, and they'll
put all that problem solving and brain focus

00:05:15.580 --> 00:05:17.470
and energy into it.

00:05:17.470 --> 00:05:21.740
Where other people are just casual, there's
not you know it's not a big thing they just

00:05:21.740 --> 00:05:25.830
want to play, so yeah, they're not going to
get that return.

00:05:25.830 --> 00:05:32.610
As much as it's easy to label mechanical skill
as a natural-born talent, it's far more empowering

00:05:32.610 --> 00:05:37.620
to know that with a complete commitment to
aim training, you're able to see a massive

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:39.360
improvement in your skill.

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:45.170
But of course, this result doesn't come with
a casual mindset; it requires that you commit

00:05:45.170 --> 00:05:46.600
yourself wholeheartedly.

00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:52.120
You must be willing to not only invest the
time but creatively think about how you can

00:05:52.120 --> 00:05:54.880
push your skills further!

00:05:54.880 --> 00:06:00.700
So consider if you've been casually approaching
your aiming skills or if you really are dedicated

00:06:00.700 --> 00:06:02.230
to improving.

00:06:02.230 --> 00:06:09.300
This mindset shift is the first step to making
breakthroughs in your skill development.

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:13.340
But then we run into another issue.

00:06:13.340 --> 00:06:18.430
Motivation is like getting a mushroom in Mario
Kart; it gives you a nice boost forward...

00:06:18.430 --> 00:06:22.630
But, what happens if you're giving yourself
a boost in the wrong direction?

00:06:22.630 --> 00:06:27.760
In other words, you might have the motivation
and drive to improve, you might be putting

00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:31.340
in the effort and time, but you're not making
progress.

00:06:31.340 --> 00:06:37.419
And the reason this is likely true is that
you're not training the right TYPE of aim.

00:06:37.419 --> 00:06:42.710
In general, we can define aim as your level
of control over the mouse.

00:06:42.710 --> 00:06:47.290
As you play the game and play aim-trainers,
your brain naturally learns how much muscular

00:06:47.290 --> 00:06:52.620
contraction is needed in your hands, wrist,
and arm to move the mouse to the appropriate

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:57.090
spot on the mouse pad and thus the proper
place on the screen.

00:06:57.090 --> 00:07:02.900
But to train the right parts of your overall
aiming skill, we need to break it down into

00:07:02.900 --> 00:07:03.900
sub-skills.

00:07:03.900 --> 00:07:07.970
RRKIM: I like to think about it in distances.

00:07:07.970 --> 00:07:15.050
So if you're aiming just a small box around
the crosshair, uh, it's what I call micromotion.

00:07:15.050 --> 00:07:20.580
So there's not much wrist or elbow or forearm
involved but just more of your hands and fingers

00:07:20.580 --> 00:07:22.320
like precision stuff.

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:26.900
And then beyond those microbox would be what
I call flicking.

00:07:26.900 --> 00:07:31.970
So just gradually building out um from distances
so if you do 180s and stuff it's what I call

00:07:31.970 --> 00:07:36.550
swiping because you start-up to now you have
to aim beyond your wrist flick, so you're

00:07:36.550 --> 00:07:39.110
using your forearm and elbow.

00:07:39.110 --> 00:07:43.000
And then there's vertical motion, so when
you aim up and down, it's completely different

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:48.810
biomechanics compared to flicking or swiping,
which is naturally anatomically easier like

00:07:48.810 --> 00:07:51.830
going left to right then is up and down like
shifting the elbow.

00:07:51.830 --> 00:07:57.669
So using distances and the biomechanics needed
for those distances, we can better understand

00:07:57.669 --> 00:07:58.669
aiming.

00:07:58.669 --> 00:08:02.710
With Ron's break down, we have micro-motions
or finger aiming.

00:08:02.710 --> 00:08:05.970
Flicking, which is more wrist aiming.

00:08:05.970 --> 00:08:08.650
Swiping which involves more forearm and elbow.

00:08:08.650 --> 00:08:13.410
And then we have vertical aiming, which requires
shifting the whole arm up and down.

00:08:13.410 --> 00:08:17.729
Now, which of these should you be training?

00:08:17.729 --> 00:08:22.360
Well, it depends on the game you play and
your playstyle within it.

00:08:22.360 --> 00:08:27.479
For example, with games like Fortnite, Overwatch,
and arena shooters like Quake, you'll have

00:08:27.479 --> 00:08:30.250
a decent amount of vertical motion.

00:08:30.250 --> 00:08:35.089
But with games like Valorant and CSGO, you'll
have far less of it, making that aspect of

00:08:35.089 --> 00:08:37.500
aim training far less critical.

00:08:37.500 --> 00:08:42.330
Then you need to consider your playstyle - how
close are you to your enemies when you are

00:08:42.330 --> 00:08:43.599
engaging them?

00:08:43.599 --> 00:08:49.899
If you are a close-combat flanker, you may
rely a lot on flicking and swiping skills.

00:08:49.899 --> 00:08:55.160
If you are a far range click timing player
like a sniper, you may depend more on precision

00:08:55.160 --> 00:08:56.790
and finger aiming.

00:08:56.790 --> 00:09:00.139
And of course, the other main factor affecting
this is your sensitivity.

00:09:00.139 --> 00:09:05.829
With high enough sensitivity, you'll rely
more on small wrist flicks and finger movement,

00:09:05.829 --> 00:09:11.309
and with lower sensitivity, you'll spend more
time making wide flicks and swipes.

00:09:11.309 --> 00:09:16.149
So as you play your game of choice, start
to pay attention to which aspects of aim you

00:09:16.149 --> 00:09:20.759
rely on the most and which you rely on the
least.

00:09:20.759 --> 00:09:28.740
Then take the time to rank the 4 main types
of aim in order of importance for you personally.

00:09:28.740 --> 00:09:34.350
This is where I started asking Ron about more
advanced tips and advice.

