00:14:11.509 --> 00:14:15.269
Now, why is this a controversial opinion?

00:14:15.269 --> 00:14:21.170
Well, in the aim training community, there's
a hot debate around the term muscle memory.

00:14:21.170 --> 00:14:24.220
But what is muscle memory?

00:14:24.220 --> 00:14:29.699
Well, as we repeat a specific movement, our
brain learns how to coordinate our muscles

00:14:29.699 --> 00:14:32.050
to perform that action.

00:14:32.050 --> 00:14:37.549
When that movement is repeated enough times,
long-term motor memory is created for that

00:14:37.549 --> 00:14:41.439
task, allowing it to be performed with less
and less conscious effort.

00:14:41.439 --> 00:14:47.929
Such as riding a bike, typing on a keyboard,
or playing an instrument.

00:14:47.929 --> 00:14:52.829
Now, this makes sense, so why is there a debate
around it?

00:14:52.829 --> 00:14:58.619
Well, some players use the term muscle memory
to claim why their aim suddenly sucks.

00:14:58.619 --> 00:15:04.769
For example, they may play with a specific
sensitivity for years, then switch it up for

00:15:04.769 --> 00:15:06.989
a few weeks.

00:15:06.989 --> 00:15:12.049
They adapt to the new mechanics for aiming
during those few weeks, but then they might

00:15:12.049 --> 00:15:15.059
decide switchback at the end of it.

00:15:15.059 --> 00:15:20.319
Of course, it takes a little bit of time to
readjust to the old sensitivity.

00:15:20.319 --> 00:15:23.929
But let's say that they claim they never readjust.

00:15:23.929 --> 00:15:28.059
They claim that the new sensitivity experiment
overrode their brian's ability to use the

00:15:28.059 --> 00:15:31.940
past sens, and now they are permanently worse
off for it.

00:15:31.940 --> 00:15:46.759
When it comes to 
factual information, as the brain learns very

00:15:46.759 --> 00:15:52.989
similar new knowledge that is too similar
to past information, it can cause conflicts

00:15:52.989 --> 00:15:55.790
and even overwrite memories.

00:15:55.790 --> 00:16:00.850
So some players assume that our mechanics
work in similar ways, causing conflict with

00:16:00.850 --> 00:16:02.269
their aim.

00:16:02.269 --> 00:16:07.429
Fortunately, motor learning is much more flexible.

00:16:07.429 --> 00:16:12.109
When you temporarily change your sensitivity,
it doesn't delete your brain's ability to

00:16:12.109 --> 00:16:13.910
use another.

00:16:13.910 --> 00:16:19.160
So those who fear muscle memory and assume
it will lead to their downfall often claim

00:16:19.160 --> 00:16:22.860
that consistency is the only way to improve.

00:16:22.860 --> 00:16:29.749
Obviously, their fears aren't entirely relevant,
but nonetheless, their emphasis on consistency

00:16:29.749 --> 00:16:32.139
still might have value.

00:16:32.139 --> 00:16:38.430
Now Ron is also a big believer in consistency,
but not in the way that changing your sensitivity

00:16:38.430 --> 00:16:43.920
for a few days, then switching back will somehow
